#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130361 :tutorial/content "The graph of any cubic function\n\n$$\ny = f(x)= ax^3 + bx^2 + cx +d\n$$\n\nwhere $a >0$, has a characteristic inverted ${\\textsf{S}}$-shape, with $y$-values ranging from $-\\infty$ to $+\\infty$. This means that every such function has at least one real root and at most three distinct roots.\n\nFor instance, suppose that we want to plot the graph of\n\n$$\ny = x^3-3x^2 +3x-1\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find the roots, so we set $y=0$ and solve. Noting that the polynomial is a perfect cube, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{3} - 3x^{2} + 3x - 1 &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 1)^{3} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we get one real root $x=1$, which has multiplicity $3$.\n\nAt a root of multiplicity $3$, the graph becomes **tangent** (i.e., lies parallel) to the $x$-axis and crosses the $x$-axis.\n\nTherefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](images/d8/d87834ef3f7225b00d92e6051a67735cf6695c5a2eff9635c4de416d0be1577f.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "848b066b-7e36-58cf-ae75-a8e28e9d3358" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3752 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130364 :tutorial/content "The graph of any cubic function\n\n$$\ny = f(x)= ax^3 + bx^2 + cx +d\n$$\n\nwhere $a <0$, has a characteristic ${\\textsf{S}}$-shape, with $y$-values ranging from $+\\infty$ to $-\\infty$.\n\nFor instance, suppose that we want to plot the graph of\n\n$$\ny = -x^3+3x^2 -3x+1\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find the roots, so we set $y=0$ and solve. Noting that the polynomial is a perfect cube, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-x^{3} + 3x^{2} - 3x + 1 &= 0 \\\\\n(-x + 1)^{3} &= 0 \\\\\n- (x - 1)^{3} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we get one real root $x=1$, which has multiplicity $3$.\n\nAt a root of multiplicity $3$, the graph becomes tangent to the $x$-axis and crosses the $x$-axis. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](images/5c/5cd99c1f2a3a596002edc222e0d9b13969286a49361fc804fe7db8818017980b.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "73e1e583-b344-5de0-9cc8-c0ec3a475e82" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3756 :tutorial/title "Negative Cubic Curves With Only One Distinct Real Root"}]}
